      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The chemical mechanisms involve changes in the solubility and speciation of iron and manganese in response to shifts in pH. Iron, which is often bound to organic matter or present in soil oxides, readily dissolves at acidic pH levels. As pH increases, iron is converted to its more soluble forms, such as ferric iron oxides (Fe3O4) and hydroxides (e.g., Fe(OH)3), which are relatively less soluble. In contrast, manganese, which is primarily associated with manganese alginate, converges to the more soluble hydrated MnO2 phase at slightly acidic pH levels due to an isoelectric point and subsequent NH4+ and/or Na+ leaching.",
